---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LETTERS, HOLY_NAMES } from '../lib/transits.js';
---

<BaseLayout title="How to practice">
  <h1>How to practice</h1>

  <p>
    Every letter is a living entity — a version of you. The more you understand the letters
    and relate them in a shape, the more senses develop from different dimensions, and human
    experience opens up. That is the whole of this practice.
  </p>

  <p class="dim small">
    A note before anything else: the letter-to-sign and letter-to-organ correspondences below
    come from Sefer Yetzirah, chapter 5. The practice itself — how the letters are used here,
    and what they open up — is the author's own experience, not official teaching.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily practice, in three parts</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>By day, the sun's letter.</strong> Each month connects with a letter through the
    sun's sign. Find today's letter on the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`}>today's letters</a> page
    and recite it through the day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>By night, the moon's letter.</strong> Each moon sign connects with a letter too.
    At night, meditate the letter of the sign the moon is in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Through both, the day's holy name.</strong> Each weekday carries its own name —
    El on Sunday, Elohim on Monday, YHWH on Tuesday, YHWH Tzevaot on Wednesday,
    Elohim Tzevaot on Thursday, Shaddai on Friday, Adonai on Shabbat. The name is pronounced
    mentally, in the mind and heart — not spoken aloud with the mouth.
  </p>

  <h2>How to sit with a letter</h2>

  <p>
    Sit. Breathe. Recite the letter, and stay with it a while. Each letter makes an organ,
    and the organs hold memories — good and bad. As you recite, notice what comes: a place in
    the body, a person from years ago, an energy you forgot you carried. The organs cry that
    they are alive, and these are our memories.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not chase the memory and do not push it away. The letter is a tool, not an idol.
    The point is the Creator himself, not power, wealth, or status from the letters.
  </p>

  <h2>The twelve letters</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Letter</th><th>Sign</th><th>Organ</th><th>Sense</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {LETTERS.map((l) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="hebrew">{l.glyph} {l.pointed} — {l.name}</td>
          <td>{l.sign}</td>
          <td>{l.organ}</td>
          <td>{l.sense}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The holy names of the week</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Day</th><th>Name</th><th>Sefirah</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {HOLY_NAMES.map((h) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{h.day}</td>
          <td class="hebrew">{h.pointed} — {h.name}</td>
          <td>{h.sefirah}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="dim small">
    The sefirot points connect the organs and make the entity one. We are letters, and each
    letter has its own memory — as our body parts, and as our main sefirot points.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
